i$", "MATH_0004": "$I = \\Ob(\\mathcal{I})$", "MATH_0005": "$M_i$", "MATH_0006": "$$\n\\colim_\\mathcal{I} M\n=\n(\\coprod\\nolimits_{i\\in I} M_i)/\\sim\n$$", "MATH_0007": "$\\sim$", "MATH_0008": "$m_i \\sim m_{i'}$", "MATH_0009": "$m_i \\in M_i$", "MATH_0010": "$m_{i'} \\in M_{i'}$", "MATH_0011": "$M(\\phi)(m_i) = m_{i'}$", "MATH_0012": "$\\phi : i \\to i'$", "MATH_0013": "$m_i \\in M_i$", "MATH_0014": "$m_{i'} \\in M_{i'}$", "MATH_0015": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0016": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\n&\ni_1 \\ar[ld] \\ar[rd] & &\ni_3 \\ar[ld] & &\ni_{2n-1} \\ar[rd] & \\\\\ni = i_0 & &\ni_2 & &\n\\ldots & &\ni_{2n} = i'\n}\n$$", "MATH_0017": "$m_{i_j} \\in M_{i_j}$", "MATH_0018": "$M_{i_{2k-1}} \\to M_{i_{2k-2}}$", "MATH_0019": "$M_{i_{2k-1}}\n\\to M_{i_{2k}}$", "MATH_0020": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{section-directed-colimits}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0002": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0003": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0004": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0005": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:2316e10c0cc2015f24fdd4a4f7f63bcda90c396b3bb9d1bc84bacfc1f69e4a3e", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:647b21f26a2efd0a2b8ea231544bfd088186778d2619e0e4a2998cccfe3b1c88", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:457fccf39a95881fcf43d9ac6199e465c8793d7fbcc4cae50c57b48a35aac867", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nNot only do limits and colimits exist in <MATH_0001>\nbut they are also easy to describe. Namely, let <MATH_0002>, <MATH_0003> be a diagram of sets.\nDenote <MATH_0004>.\nThe limit is described as\n<MATHSEG_0001> in <MATHSEG_0002>\nSo we think of an element of the limit as a compatible system of elements\nof all the sets <MATH_0005>.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nOn the other hand, the colimit is\n<MATH_0006>\nwhere the equivalence relation <MATH_0007> is the equivalence relation\ngenerated by setting <MATH_0008> if <MATH_0009>,\n<MATH_0010> and <MATH_0011> for some\n<MATH_0012>. In other words, <MATH_0013>\nand <MATH_0014> are equivalent if there are a\nchain of morphisms in <MATH_0015>\n<MATH_0016>\nand elements <MATH_0017> mapping to each other under\nthe maps <MATH_0018> and <MATH_0019> induced from the maps in <MATH_0020> above.\n\n<STRUCT_0004><STRUCT_0005>\nThis is not a very pleasant type of object to work with.\nBut if the diagram is filtered then it is much easier to\ndescribe. We will explain this in Section <REF_0001>.\n\n\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:a1ee46edad41a68de43c15a6e0b563f9790fd90102f54ebe6b9d92c12a5d6ef8", "unit_id": "tag:002U:prose-0001"}
